WEBVTT

00:03.880 --> 00:06.520
Okay, so now let's focus more about the SQL itself.

00:06.520 --> 00:09.120
We have an SQL different type of commands.

00:09.120 --> 00:10.640
So let's say that we have a database.

00:10.640 --> 00:12.040
And this database is empty.

00:12.040 --> 00:13.440
So we have nothing inside it.

00:13.480 --> 00:19.000
Now of course the first thing that you have to do is to write an SQL with the command create in order

00:19.000 --> 00:21.800
to create brand new table in the database.

00:21.800 --> 00:24.400
So once you executed the database can go and build one.

00:24.400 --> 00:27.440
But this table is empty so we have nothing inside it.

00:27.440 --> 00:30.720
So now what you have done here is you have defined something new right.

00:30.720 --> 00:34.120
And we call this type of commands the data definition language.

00:34.120 --> 00:40.320
The DDL we have create to create something new alter in order to edit something that already exists

00:40.320 --> 00:44.080
and drop in order to delete something to drop, for example a table.

00:44.080 --> 00:46.200
So this is the first family of commands.

00:46.240 --> 00:48.160
Now if you look at our table it is empty.

00:48.200 --> 00:48.880
What do we need?

00:48.920 --> 00:49.800
We need data.

00:49.800 --> 00:52.480
So let's say that we have a website or an application.

00:52.480 --> 00:54.960
Now this application is generating a lot of data.

00:55.000 --> 01:00.920
Now in order for this application to move the data inside our new table, it must use the SQL command

01:00.920 --> 01:01.560
insert.

01:01.560 --> 01:05.700
So if you execute insert you can add new data inside your table.

01:05.700 --> 01:09.300
This type of commands, we call it data manipulation language.

01:09.300 --> 01:10.620
And here we have three commands.

01:10.620 --> 01:15.900
Insert in order to insert a new data update, in order to update an already existing data and delete

01:15.900 --> 01:18.260
in order to go and delete data from your table.

01:18.260 --> 01:22.860
And that's why we call it data manipulation language, because you are manipulating your data.

01:22.860 --> 01:23.660
So what do you have now?

01:23.660 --> 01:24.380
We have table.

01:24.380 --> 01:26.060
We have data inside the table.

01:26.100 --> 01:28.820
Now what we can do we can start asking questions.

01:28.820 --> 01:31.660
So let's say that you have analytical question about your data.

01:31.700 --> 01:35.100
Now all what you have to do is to write something called SQL query.

01:35.100 --> 01:37.580
And inside it you use the command select.

01:37.580 --> 01:39.340
But the whole thing we call it a query.

01:39.340 --> 01:45.580
So you send a query to the database, you have a question and the database can return for you the result

01:45.580 --> 01:48.420
that data answering your query or question.

01:48.420 --> 01:53.220
And we call this type of activities using SQL the data query language.

01:53.260 --> 01:54.500
And here we have only one.

01:54.500 --> 01:55.940
And it is very famous.

01:55.980 --> 01:57.220
We have the select.

01:57.220 --> 01:59.620
We can use it in order to query our data.

01:59.620 --> 02:02.620
So those are the three different commands in SQL.

02:02.660 --> 02:04.260
And of course we're going to learn all of them.

02:04.260 --> 02:10.500
But we will spend most of our time learning how to write the correct query for the correct answer.
